{"agent":"Monster3-RC-tree-result-cache-7267fe","data":{"by":"Qwen/Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-FP8","evidence":{"chunk_findings":[{"chunk":2,"detail":"The cache key is built from the unresolved `gdef.command`, while the command that actually runs is produced later by `WK.resolve(...)`. If `WK.resolve` expands or changes the command (for example, a gate command containing `auto` becomes `-n 1` in one environment and `-n 8` in another, or suite-holding changes the resolved command/timeout), two runs with different effective commands get the same `rc_key`. A stored result from the first run can therefore be returned for the second run without executing the different command, despite the comment claiming the hit is for the same command.","digest":"a9356abf4a3667ab","family":"alibaba","location":"ddflow/services/gates/runner.py:302","malformed":"","reviewer":"[REDACTED:name]","severity":"MEDIUM","title":"ddflow/services/gates/runner.py:302"},{"chunk":3,"detail":"A dirty submodule is removed from the cache key instead of being represented. `git diff HEAD --name-only` reports a modified submodule as a path, but the code then checks `path.is_file()` and, because the path is a directory, executes `entries.pop(rel, None)` without adding any worktree entry. Thus the parent HEAD gitlink entry is erased and the submodule commit is not included in `code_key`. Given a HEAD that contains submodule `lib` at commit `X`, a run with `lib` checked out at commit `Y` can store a passed result; a later run with `lib` checked out at a different commit `Z` computes the same code key because `lib` is absent from the hashed rows, so the cache serves the old passed result even though the tree differs.","digest":"fd96a1247aa41007","family":"alibaba","location":"ddflow/services/resultcache.py:_code_entries","malformed":"","reviewer":"[REDACTED:name]","severity":"HIGH","title":"ddflow/services/resultcache.py:_code_entries"},{"chunk":3,"detail":"The key does not include the version of the resolved gate command, only the command string. For example, with the same tree, the same command `\"pytest\"`, no lockfile change, and no `resultcache.env_vars` entry capturing the tool version or `PATH`, upgrading the global `pytest` executable from 7.x to 8.x leaves `_python()`, `sys.platform`, `_env()`, `tree`, and `command` unchanged. `key()` therefore returns the previous key, and a cached passed result from the old tool version is served for the new tool version.","digest":"791505354742201a","family":"alibaba","location":"ddflow/services/resultcache.py:key","malformed":"","reviewer":"[REDACTED:name]","severity":"MEDIUM","title":"ddflow/services/resultcache.py:key"},{"chunk":3,"detail":"An unreadable lockfile is silently omitted rather than causing a cache miss. If `Path(cwd)/name` exists but `p.read_text(...)` raises `PermissionError` or another `OSError`, the `contextlib.suppress(OSError)` skips the append entirely, so the lockfile contributes no part to the digest. With `uv.lock` present but unreadable by the cache process, two runs with different lockfile contents can produce the same key, allowing the second run to hit the first run's passed entry even though the dependency lock file changed and was not read back whole.","digest":"8f2377dd91aaf43c","family":"alibaba","location":"ddflow/services/resultcache.py:_env","malformed":"","reviewer":"[REDACTED:name]","severity":"MEDIUM","title":"ddflow/services/resultcache.py:_env"},{"chunk":3,"detail":"For changed or untracked files, the code stores only `f\"worktree {blob}\"`, dropping the file mode. Clean HEAD entries include mode, but worktree entries do not. Given an untracked file `run.sh` with identical contents but mode `755` in one run and mode `644` in another, `_code_entries` produces the same entry and `code_key` is identical. If the gate command invokes `./run.sh`, the first run may pass while the second would fail because the file is not executable, but the cache reuses the passed result.","digest":"6dddf95d07438ff3","family":"alibaba","location":"ddflow/services/resultcache.py:_code_entries","malformed":"","reviewer":"[REDACTED:name]","severity":"MEDIUM","title":"ddflow/services/resultcache.py:_code_entries"},{"chunk":4,"detail":"`resultcache.enabled` is added with `new = 'true'`, so an upgrade turns the cache on for existing projects, while the documented key (`ddflow/templates/prompts/help/parallel.md:117`) only hashes tracked files, the gate, the command string, the Python version, dependency lock files, and `[resultcache].env_vars` (default `[]`). Given a project with no dependency lock files and a `ci` gate whose command is `pytest`, run it once in an environment with pytest 7.4 and it passes; the result is cached. Then change only the environment/tool version, e.g. `pip install --upgrade pytest` to 8.0 or switch `PATH` to another virtualenv where the tests fail. The tracked files, gate name, command string, Python version, lock files, and `env_vars` are unchanged, so the second run is served `passed` from cache. This reports a failed gate as passed; the key must include the resolved interpreter/tool version (or full environment), or the feature must not default to enabled.","digest":"93cb6d27234d1553","family":"alibaba","location":"ddflow/templates/upgrade/unreleased/knob_added.resultcache.enabled.toml:4","malformed":"","reviewer":"[REDACTED:name]","severity":"HIGH","title":"ddflow/templates/upgrade/unreleased/knob_added.resultcache.enabled.toml:4"},{"chunk":6,"detail":"The `git` helper is not hermetic: it copies the ambient environment and only overrides author/committer identity. If a developer’s global Git config enables signing (`commit.gpgsign=true`) or hooks (`core.hooksPath`), the `git commit -q -m init` calls in `project()` and `commit()` can fail, causing every test that creates a repository to error. For example, with a global config containing `commit.gpgsign=true` and no usable signing key, `git(repo, \"commit\", ...)` raises `CalledProcessError`. The helper should isolate Git config (e.g. `GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL=os.devnull`, `GIT_CONFIG_SYSTEM=os.devnull`) or pass `--no-gpg-sign --no-verify`.","digest":"fca964095def8414","family":"alibaba","location":"tests/test_result_cache.py:18-28","malformed":"","reviewer":"[REDACTED:name]","severity":"MEDIUM","title":"tests/test_result_cache.py:18-28"},{"chunk":6,"detail":"`test_a_worktree_shares_the_projects_cache` only asserts that `RC.entry_path(repo, \"k\").parent == RC.entry_path(wt, \"k\").parent`. That proves the worktree resolves to the same cache directory, but it does not prove cache results are shared. If `RC.key` includes the absolute worktree path (or the worktree’s branch), the repo and linked worktree will produce different keys for the same tree, so a result stored from the repo will never be looked up from the worktree; the test still passes because both `entry_path` calls have the same parent directory.","digest":"f829ad98f658e1f8","family":"alibaba","location":"tests/test_result_cache.py:192-196","malformed":"","reviewer":"[REDACTED:name]","severity":"MEDIUM","title":"tests/test_result_cache.py:192-196"},{"chunk":6,"detail":"The gate commands embed `tmp_path` unquoted into shell redirection (`echo ran >> {tmp_path}/...`). If pytest’s temporary directory path contains spaces (common on Windows user profiles, or on POSIX systems with a spaced `TMPDIR`), the shell splits the redirection target. For example, with `tmp_path=/tmp/pytest 123/test`, the command becomes `echo ran >> /tmp/pytest 123/test/count.txt`, which redirects to `/tmp/pytest` and then tries to execute `123/test/count.txt`, so the gate command fails or writes the wrong file and the test fails. The paths should be shell-quoted.","digest":"c4b848b1f3bb0c67","family":"alibaba","location":"tests/test_result_cache.py:252,272","malformed":"","reviewer":"[REDACTED:name]","severity":"LOW","title":"tests/test_result_cache.py:252,272"},{"chunk":7,"detail":"The test starts a thread that is expected to acquire the queued lock and append `\"agent-y\"` to `got`, then calls `t.join(10)`, but it never asserts `got` or that the thread finished. If the lock release path fails to wake the queued holder, or the thread times out/raises after creating the ticket, the ticket note can still contain `class: integrator` and the final assertion passes even though the queued hold never acquired the lock. For example, if `SL.hold` writes the queued ticket with the class but does not release the lock to the waiting thread, this test still succeeds.","digest":"1ace1830f6894152","family":"alibaba","location":"tests/test_suite_queue_priority.py:test_a_block_can_queue_its_holds_as_a_class","malformed":"","reviewer":"[REDACTED:name]","severity":"LOW","title":"tests/test_suite_queue_priority.py:test_a_block_can_queue_its_holds_as_a_class"}],"chunks_total":7,"coverage":"7/7 chunk(s) reviewed","delta_rounds":0,"diff_chars":36093,"diff_sha":"19be10566a05097a7d3e341b2de7272bbe564be360285123d916142a54b6a878","diff_source":"main..HEAD + tracked working-tree changes in $HOME/src/ddflow/.ddflow/worktrees/RC-tree-result-cache","diff_stat":{"deletions":0,"files":0,"insertions":0,"untracked":0},"elapsed_s":557.1,"excluded_bytes":1184,"excluded_files":1,"excluded_paths":["README.md"],"family":"alibaba","findings":10,"max_chunk_chars":5000,"model":"[REDACTED:name]","output_digest":"c14caa559c107023","output_file":".ddflow/local/reviews/RC-tree-result-cache.rubber_duck.[REDACTED:name].20261112T174730Z-86f7274c.jsonl","reason":"","review_degraded":[{"at":1794506208.214312,"family":"google","gate":"rubber_duck","model":"gemini-3.5-flash-lite","reason":"HTTP 429","reviewer":"gemini-3.5-flash-lite"},{"at":1794506208.4995914,"family":"xai","gate":"rubber_duck","model":"grok-4.7","reason":"HTTP 403","reviewer":"grok-4.7"}],"review_kind":"full","reviewed":[1,2,3,4,5,6,7],"reviewed_for_holder":"[REDACTED:hostname]-RC-tree-result-cache-7267fe reviewed lane-sl-throughput's RC-tree-result-cache","reviewed_head":"ebc2f3acaa9160